# NO trailing slash, deliberately. `node_modules/` matches a DIRECTORY and not a SYMLINK, so
# it never matched the node_modules symlink this repo used to track — which checked itself
# out as a link to itself and made the TypeScript compiler unreachable. The bare name matches
# both the directory and a symlink of that name.
node_modules
dist/
*.tgz
bun.lock
bb-ts-core.wasm
.bb-data/
bb.json
.bb_backups/

# Local secrets (never commit)
.env.local
.env*.local

# website/ (absorbed from a nested repo) — mirror its local ignores
website/.next/
website/.vercel/
website/.astro/
website/*.log
website/*.tsbuildinfo
website/.env.vercel

# VSIX build artifacts (packaging evidence records the artifact, not the blob)
*.vsix
.p3-runner.tmp.ts
tool-mode-corpus.json.fresh
.tools/

# @vscode/test-electron download cache (R3/M25 e2e artifacts)
.vscode-test/

# client-tooling client runtime dir
.client-tooling/

# tree-sitter grammar build outputs. `tree-sitter generate` writes a ~16MB
# parser.c and the node-types/grammar JSON, and `tree-sitter build` writes a
# local wasm; only the hand-written grammar sources (grammar.js, package.json,
# tree-sitter.json, README, scanner.h) are tracked. The wasm the product loads
# is the one committed under wasm/, which is the artifact the evidence names.
# NOTE: the pattern excludes the CONTENTS of src/, not the directory, because
# git cannot re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded -- the
# scanner.c negation below is inert if this reads `src/`.
grammars/tree-sitter-*/src/*
grammars/tree-sitter-*/tree-sitter-*.wasm
# ...EXCEPT hand-written external scanners, which are SOURCE, not build output.
# The set of grammar source files that must be tracked for a rebuild is named in
# the comment above as "grammar.js, package.json, tree-sitter.json, README,
# scanner.h" -- and `scanner.h` covers the headers an in-tree scanner dir holds.
# tree-sitter-cpp's external scanner is `src/scanner.c`, so the blanket `src/`
# ignore silently dropped the one file its grammar cannot be rebuilt without.
# Found while vendoring tree-sitter-cpp for #743: the ignore list named the
# scanner by its C++-in-fsharp-style filename instead of by its role.
!grammars/tree-sitter-cpp/src/scanner.c
# Same trap, second grammar: tree-sitter-css ships a hand-written external scanner
# (src/scanner.c — DESCENDANT_OP, PSEUDO_CLASS_SELECTOR_COLON, ERROR_RECOVERY).
# Without it the tracked tree cannot rebuild the wasm, and the wired wasm is a
# LOCAL build of grammars/tree-sitter-css/grammar.js (two patched hunks), so the
# scanner is now load-bearing for CSS exactly as it is for cpp. Found while
# vendoring tree-sitter-css for the CSS lane's url(../..) parse class.
!grammars/tree-sitter-css/src/scanner.c
# Fourth grammar, same trap: tree-sitter-scss's external scanner implements the
# `CONCAT` token, which is what `_concatenated_identifier` is built from — SCSS
# interpolation such as `#{$a}-suffix`. Without this negation the file is silently
# untracked, the grammar still generates (scanner.c is regenerated? no — it is NOT
# generated, it is hand-written, and its ABSENCE makes the build fail at link time
# with an undefined `_concat` rather than at parse time), and the lane looks vendored.
!grammars/tree-sitter-scss/src/scanner.c

# Grammar registry network cache (M60). Raw npm/GitHub answers, ~3.6 MB,
# regenerable with `bun run grammar:registry:refresh`, and deliberately NOT
# committed: the registry it produces IS committed and IS gated. Committing the
# cache would make a stale upstream answer look like a pinned fact.
validation/m60/grammar-survey.cache.json
# Third grammar, same trap: tree-sitter-html's tag vocabulary and its external
# scanner are BOTH hand-written source (`src/tag.h` is the 126-entry tag table the
# scanner looks names up in, and `src/scanner.c` is what tokenises tag names at
# all) — without them the tracked tree cannot rebuild the wasm, and the tag table
# is exactly the file that decides whether `<LINK>` is recognised as a void
# element. Found while vendoring tree-sitter-html for #288: the html lane's whole
# case/void behaviour is a property of these two files, so a vendored copy that
# omitted them would be provenance theatre. NEITHER is modified from upstream —
# the committed wasm was measured to behave identically to a fresh build of this
# exact source — so this vendoring exists to make the provenance checkable, not to
# carry a patch.
!grammars/tree-sitter-html/src/scanner.c
!grammars/tree-sitter-html/src/tag.h

# FOURTH grammar, the same trap a fourth time — and this is the evidence that the
# rule above is wrong rather than merely incomplete. The negations are written by
# FILENAME (`src/scanner.c`, `src/tag.h`), and every one of these four grammars
# happens to name its hand-written scanner `src/scanner.c`. A grammar whose
# scanner were named anything else — `src/lexer.c`, `src/tokenizer.cpp` — would
# be dropped silently, and the failure mode is the worst kind: the tracked tree
# still LOOKS complete (grammar.js is there, so `tree-sitter generate` succeeds),
# it rebuilds a wasm that is missing its external tokens, and the result is a
# grammar that parses most files and silently mis-lexes the ones the scanner
# exists for. The right fix is to negate by ROLE — track the whole hand-written
# src/ for any grammar that has one — which is what `!grammars/tree-sitter-*/src/
# scanner.*` below does, keeping the generated parser.c/grammar.json/node-types
# .json ignored. Do that migration deliberately, per grammar, with the A/B to
# back it; the four negations above stay until each one is replaced.
!grammars/tree-sitter-powershell/src/scanner.c
# tree-sitter-powershell's own note, recorded because it is the kind of thing
# that looks fine and is not: the grammar's `tree-sitter.json` declares
# file-types ["ps1", "psm1"] and does NOT declare .psd1, yet the product indexes
# .psd1 as PowerShell and 24 of the corpus files in this lane's ansible draw are
# .psd1 module manifests full of here-strings and DSC configuration. The grammar
# parses them; the manifest simply never claimed them. Undeclared-by-the-grammar
# is not unparsed-by-the-grammar, and this lane's measurements are taken over all
# three extensions precisely because the declaration and the product disagree.

# FIFTH grammar, and the first to need the role-based form the FOURTH comment above
# asks for. tree-sitter-yaml (0.7.1) does not have ONE hand-written external
# scanner, it has FOUR: src/scanner.c plus the three mutually exclusive scalar
# backends src/schema.core.c, src/schema.json.c and src/schema.legacy.c, which
# scanner.c selects between at build time --
#
#     #ifndef YAML_SCHEMA
#     #define YAML_SCHEMA core
#     #endif
#     #include _file(YAML_SCHEMA)     // -> src/schema.<YAML_SCHEMA>.c
#
# So a filename-based negation would have to name all four by hand, and would still
# miss the next grammar that splits its scanner. This entry therefore negates by
# ROLE as the FOURTH comment prescribes: track the whole hand-written src/, and
# re-ignore only what `tree-sitter generate` writes.
#
# Why the generated JSONs stay TRACKED here, unlike haskell/elixir where the
# blanket rule leaves them untracked: the pin convention's sixth component is the
# offline source<->wasm proof, and it runs on src/node-types.json and
# src/grammar.json. A fresh clone that has grammar.js but not node-types.json can
# `tree-sitter generate` a working parser and cannot run the proof -- the tracked
# tree would LOOK complete and the provenance claim would be unverifiable, which
# is the same failure mode the FOURTH comment describes. They are 166 KB total.
# src/parser.c (1.29 MB) remains ignored: it is regenerated from grammar.js.
#
# MECHANICAL NOTE, because the first attempt at this entry was WRONG in a way that
# looked right. `git add -n` accepted it and took five files. The pattern that
# needed undoing was `grammars/tree-sitter-*/src/*`, which matches the CONTENTS of
# src/, and a directory-shaped negation `!grammars/tree-sitter-yaml/src/` does not
# undo it: gitignore(5) evaluates each path independently, so re-including the
# directory leaves every file inside still matched by the `src/*` rule. The negation
# has to be role-shaped at the same LEVEL as the rule it undoes -- `src/*` in, so
# `src/*` out -- and then the one generated file re-ignored individually below.
# Verified with `git add -n` and `git check-ignore -v`, not by reading the diff.
!grammars/tree-sitter-yaml/src/*
grammars/tree-sitter-yaml/src/parser.c

# --- load-test output written to the repository ROOT, and scratch files. These are not
# work: a load test prints its per-entry lines to bare filenames, and `git add -A` will
# happily commit them. The same hazard already forced an untrack of a node_modules
# symlink, so it is closed here at the source rather than one file at a time.
/2300
/400
/600
/Runnable
/validation/m14/results/*.json.tmp-*
/script/m60/tmp-*.ts

# `.merge-tmp/` is a REGISTERED git worktree (branch main-align). It appeared here because a
# worktree lives inside the repository; committing it would nest a repository in this one.
/.merge-tmp/

